<p>Ford Foundation has title, copyright and literary rights in the collection, in so far as it holds them.</p><p>The Rockefeller Archive Center has authority to grant permission to cite and publish material from the collection. Permission to publish extensive excerpts, or material in its entirety, will be referred to the Ford Foundation.</p>  </userestrict>
  <arrangement id="c578d7697d11e6c871229b80cca93786">
    <head>Arrangement</head>
<p>Collection consists of two series. The series are based on file type.</p><p>
Original order was maintained during processing.</p><p>
Collection arrangement is as follows:</p><p>
Series 1. Chronological files, 1987</p><p>
Series 2. Subject files, 1983-1988</p>  </arrangement>
  <bioghist id="c04234fa0682a1e411f3c83cfac24e2a">
    <head>Biographical / Historical</head>
<p>Stephen P. Marks worked as a Program Officer in the Ford Foundation's Human Rights and Governance Program (HRG) between 1984 and 1989. He was responsible for grant-making decisions in the area of international human rights.</p><p>Marks received his BA from Stanford University in 1964 and a Masters degree from the Institute of Advanced International Studies of the University of Law, Economics, and Social Sciences of Paris in 1971. Prior to joining the Ford Foundation, he worked at the International Institute of Human Rights from between 1969 and 1973 and the United Nations Educational, Scientific, and Cultural Organization (UNESCO) from between 1973 and 1984. After he left Ford in 1989, he continued to pursue a career in international human rights philanthropy and research with positions at the United Nations, Princeton University, and Columbia University among other institutions, and then in 2000, he became the Director of the Harvard T.H. Chan School of Public Health Program on Human Rights in Development 
  </p>  </bioghist>
