  <dsc><c id="f48bf685e553d920143dc7c99de28963" level="series"><did><unittitle>Chronological files</unittitle><unitid>1</unitid><unitid type="aspace_uri">/repositories/2/archival_objects/1079160</unitid><physdesc altrender="whole"><extent altrender="materialtype spaceoccupied">0.38 Cubic Feet</extent><extent altrender="carrier">1 LTR size Hollinger box</extent></physdesc><unitdate datechar="creation" normal="1987/1987">1987</unitdate></did><scopecontent id="ce457b2324d4b5ddbf69c4b275640ac0"><head>Scope and Contents</head><p>Series contains the chronological files Stephen P. Marks maintained when he worked as a Program Officer in the Ford Foundation's Human Rights and Governance Program (HRG). It currently consists of only one file that was created in 1987, but it may expand if the RAC receives more accessions of Marks' Ford records.</p><p>
It is chiefly comprised of correspondence and internal Ford memos that Marks sent to human rights organizations and Ford staff members. However, it also contains some correspondence that was addressed to Marks by the aforementioned entities as well as other kinds of documents such as meeting agendas and project proposals. Marks frequently corresponded with Human Rights and Governance Program Director Shepard Forman as well as with organizations like the International League for Human Rights, the International Human Rights Law Group, and the Lawyers Committee for Human Rights. Correspondence discusses subjects like measures against torture, the rights of minorities, women and indigenous peoples, democracy, and international human rights law.</p><p>
Original order was maintained and files represent how Marks compiled the materials. Researchers will therefore see the majority of correspondence in reverse chronological order of creation date.</p></scopecontent><c id="6f136a328f4119e9db456a416ec7590f" level="file"><did><unittitle>Chronological file</unittitle><unitid type="aspace_uri">/repositories/2/archival_objects/1079162</unitid><unitdate datechar="creation" normal="1987/1987">1987</unitdate><langmaterial id="c9a7b7c9fe88aa5a25831807b4d88432">Materials predominately in English. File also contains many letters in French and a few in Spanish.</langmaterial><container altrender="Letter Document Box" id="29ab527ad08a429b41947940e836fd27" label="mixed materials [A0000000017427]" type="box">1</container><container id="0f34119bc1a55e2eb4cbaca2608eef65" parent="29ab527ad08a429b41947940e836fd27" type="folder">1-8</container></did></c></c><c id="9420bfc8da9e9e381f2784e3ab0a9526" level="series"><did><unittitle>Subject files</unittitle><unitid>2</unitid><unitid type="aspace_uri">/repositories/2/archival_objects/1143782</unitid><physdesc altrender="whole"><extent altrender="materialtype spaceoccupied">1.14 Cubic Feet</extent><extent altrender="carrier">3 LTR size Hollinger boxes</extent></physdesc><unitdate datechar="creation" normal="1983/1988" type="inclusive">1983-1988</unitdate></did><scopecontent id="50809aa787b2f9a020f31261bad3ce81"><head>Scope and Contents</head><p>Series contains the subject files Stephen P. Marks maintained when he worked as a Program Officer in the Ford Foundation's Human Rights and Governance Program (HRG). Files document the Human Rights and Governance Program's collaboration with and grant support to human rights organizations and projects in Africa, the Middle East, Asia, and South America during the 1980s. These organizations and projects focused on issues such as the development of human rights law, protections, education, and resources in countries undergoing development, rights protections for different types of groups such as indigenous peoples and returning exiles, research on human rights issues in different countries, and the role of lawyers and judiciaries in the adoption and application of human rights law. Several of the files regard Marks' trips abroad to countries like Israel, Egypt, Chile, and Uruguay where he met with local and regional human rights organizations and institutions. Documents show that Marks worked with the Ford Foundation Field Offices in Cairo, Lima, and Nairobi to set up these trips as well as on other matters.</p></scopecontent><c id="c453325e66524794c6e6e8ad27e1f303" level="file"><did><unittitle>Africa</unittitle><unitid type="aspace_uri">/repositories/2/archival_objects/1143784</unitid><unitdate datechar="creation" normal="1983/1988" type="inclusive">1983-1988</unitdate><langmaterial id="e3bc2625e5817b04eb6bb4d7965ca5c4">Materials predominately in English. File also contains some documents in French.</langmaterial><container altrender="Letter Document Box" id="af1f0b5c7c9bc548ee674f3cad591d2b" label="mixed materials [A0000000114125]" type="box">2</container><container id="eb4654a543a1ec915bb4edcc8c32f7e0" parent="af1f0b5c7c9bc548ee674f3cad591d2b" type="folder">9-10</container></did></c><c id="0fe228d12b913c237a13899e1311ad16" level="file"><did><unittitle>Africa</unittitle><unitid type="aspace_uri">/repositories/2/archival_objects/1143785</unitid><unitdate datechar="creation" normal="1983/1984" type="inclusive">1983-1984</unitdate><container altrender="Letter Document Box" id="93943652ba3dc2accc0567b025b8e384" label="mixed materials [A0000000114125]" type="box">2</container><container id="4ba6cb10bc0c7a24da97a06fc447c34f" parent="93943652ba3dc2accc0567b025b8e384" type="folder">11</container></did></c><c id="05ab27c59b6e7aa630dbb324f06b31f8" level="file"><did><unittitle>African Jurists Association (AJA)</unittitle><unitid type="aspace_uri">/repositories/2/archival_objects/1143787</unitid><unitdate datechar="creation" no